config KFEAT_DRIVER_RAMDISK_STATIC
    bool "Back the RAM disk with an embedded filesystem image"
    depends on KFEAT_DRIVER_RAMDISK
    help
      Populate the RAM disk from a build-time filesystem image embedded into
      the kernel, instead of an empty zero-filled region, so it can be mounted
      as a real (read-write) root filesystem. The image path is set by the
      RAMDISK_IMG Makefile variable (default: an empty ext4 image produced by
      'make ramdisk_img'); any ext4 image may be substituted. Select the
      matching filesystem support (KFEAT_FS_EXT4 by default; also enable
      KFEAT_FS_FAT if a FAT image is used). The image is embedded in .data, so
      the kernel binary grows by roughly the image size.

comment "Network drivers"


config KFEAT_DRIVER_VIRTIO_RNG
    bool "virtio-rng hardware driver"
    default y if MACHINE_AARCH64_QEMU
    default y if MACHINE_RISCV64_QEMU
    default y if MACHINE_X86_64_QEMU
    default y if MACHINE_LOONGARCH64_QEMU
    help
      Enable the virtio-rng hardware entropy source driver. Required on
      virtio-based virtual platforms (QEMU/CrosVM) when guest randomness should
      come from the VMM-provided entropy device. Enable
      KFEAT_ENTROPY_VIRTIO_RNG separately to connect it to the entropy pool.

config KFEAT_ENTROPY_VIRTIO_RNG
    bool "Use VirtIO RNG as a trusted entropy source"
    depends on KFEAT_DRIVER_VIRTIO_RNG
    help
      Connect discovered VirtIO RNG devices to the kernel entropy pool through
      the Hwrng class. The driver can be enabled independently of this consumer.
      Enabling this trusts the VMM / host: its bytes are mixed into the pool
      and may establish random-number readiness without another entropy source.
      Disable this for isolated deployments that must not trust host entropy;
      the hardware driver can remain enabled independently.

config KFEAT_ENTROPY_ARCH_CPU
    bool "CPU instruction RNG (AArch64 RNDR / x86 RDSEED·RDRAND)"
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64 || ARCH_X86_64
    help
      Mix entropy from architecture CPU RNG instructions when available:
        - AArch64: RNDR / RNDRRS (Armv8.5 RNG extension)
        - x86_64: RDSEED preferred, RDRAND as fallback

      Defaults to n on architectures without a CPU RNG instruction path
      (for example RISC-V today). Kept visible on all arches so shared
      kernel crates can always reference the Kconfig constant.

      This path does not trust the VMM and is preferred on bare-metal or
      hardware-isolated deployments. VirtIO RNG remains available as a
      fallback when this option is disabled or the CPU lacks the extension
      (some QEMU CPU models).

config KFEAT_ENTROPY_SMCCC_TRNG
    bool "SMCCC firmware TRNG (ARM TRNG SMCCC 1.0)"
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64
    help
      Mix entropy from the ARM SMCCC TRNG firmware service (TRNG_RND64) when
      EL3/TF-A exposes it. Probe fails silently on platforms without the
      service, such as typical QEMU virt guests. Does not require Device Tree
      and is suitable for ACPI bare-metal platforms.

      Defaults to n on non-AArch64 architectures. Kept visible on all arches
      so shared kernel crates can always reference the Kconfig constant.

config KFEAT_ENTROPY_JITTER
    bool "Software jitter entropy (timer / interrupt timing noise)"
    default y
    help
      Mix low-rate entropy collected from timing variations during busy waits.
      Interrupts, scheduling, and cache effects perturb the measured intervals.
      Used as a fallback when HRNG sources are unavailable, and as
      supplementary input when they are.

